    <help format="markdown"><![CDATA[
**stress-ng** — Stress test a computer system in various selectable ways

stress-ng was originally intended to make a machine work hard and trip hardware
issues such as thermal overruns as well as operating system bugs that only
occur when a system is being thrashed hard. Use stress-ng with caution as some
of the tests can make a system run hot on poorly designed hardware and also can
cause excessive system thrashing which may be difficult to stop.

stress-ng can also measure test throughput rates; this can be useful to observe
performance changes across different operating system releases or types of
hardware. However, it has never been intended to be used as a precise benchmark
test suite, so do **not** use it in this manner.

-----

**Common options** provides quick access to the most frequently used stressors
(CPU, disk, I/O, virtual memory) with sensible defaults.

**All options** exposes additional stressors (fork, iomix, cyclic latency) and
control parameters such as CPU affinity pinning, metrics, and timing statistics.

**Run duration** controls how long the test runs. Durations accept second (s),
minute (m), hour (h), day (d) and year (y) suffixes. Start with short durations
(e.g. 10 seconds) to verify your system handles the load before running longer
tests.

**Bogo-operations** are a stress-ng concept: each stressor defines its own unit
of work (a "bogo-operation"). The `--metrics` flag reports how many
bogo-operations per second each stressor achieved, which can be useful for
relative performance comparisons.

**CPU stress methods** select the kind of computation performed by the CPU
stressors, e.g. `fft`, `matrixprod`, `prime` or a mixture of all methods. The
set of available methods changed in stress-ng 0.22.01; run
`stress-ng --cpu-method bogus` to have the tool print the full list of choices
supported by the installed version.

**Privileged stressors.** The cyclic stressor uses a real-time scheduling policy
by default, which requires the CAP_SYS_NICE capability. Inside containers and
other unprivileged environments the stressor is then skipped and the run ends
with a warning. Select a non-real-time cyclic scheduling policy (`other`,
`batch` or `idle`) to measure latencies without any special privileges.

-----

**Examples**

`--cpu 4 --io 2 --vm 1 --vm-bytes 1G --timeout 60s`
  Run for 60 seconds with 4 CPU stressors, 2 I/O stressors, and 1 VM stressor
  using 1 GB of memory.

`--vm 8 --vm-bytes 80% --timeout 1h`
  Run 8 virtual memory stressors that combined use 80% of available memory for
  1 hour. Each stressor uses 10% of available memory.

`--cpu 4 --cpu-method fft --cpu-ops 10000 --metrics-brief`
  Run 4 FFT CPU stressors, stop after 10000 bogo-operations, and produce a brief
  metrics summary.

`--cyclic 1 --cyclic-policy other --timeout 10s`
  Measure scheduling latencies for 10 seconds without needing CAP_SYS_NICE.

`--fork 4 --timeout 30s`
  Run 4 fork stressors for 30 seconds to exercise process creation and
  destruction performance.

Project: https://github.com/ColinIanKing/stress-ng
    ]]></help>
